The '''2022 UEFA Champions League final''' was the final match of the [[2021–22 UEFA Champions League]], the 67th season of Europe's premier club football tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the 30th season since it was renamed from the [[European Champion Clubs' Cup]] to the [[UEFA Champions League]]. It was played at the [[Stade de France]] in [[Saint-Denis, Seine-Saint-Denis|Saint-Denis]], France, on 28 May 2022, between English club [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] and Spanish club [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]]. It was the third time the two sides have met in the European Cup final, after [[1981 European Cup final|1981]] and [[2018 UEFA Champions League final|2018]], the third final held at the Stade de France, after the [[2000 UEFA Champions League final|2000]] and [[2006 UEFA Champions League final|2006]] finals, and the first time the same two teams have met in three finals.<ref>{{cite web |title=Liverpool vs Real Madrid Champions League final match facts: Previous meetings, pedigree, links and trivia, latest news |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0275-15281eca12ba-8c4b2a20b3c3-1000--liverpool-vs-real-madrid-champions-league-final-match-facts-pre/ |website=UEFA.com|date=28 May 2022 |access-date=29 May 2022}}</ref> Real Madrid won the match 1–0 via a 59th-minute goal from [[Vinícius Júnior]], assisted by [[Federico Valverde]], for [[European Cup and UEFA Champions League records and statistics#General performances|a record-extending 14th title]], and their fifth in nine years.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0275-15415a62aaf0-36d3e17d7404-1000/ |title=Champions League final: Vinícius Júnior scores only goal as Real Madrid beat Liverpool to claim 14th European Cup |work=UEFA.com |date=28 May 2022 |access-date=28 May 2022}}</ref>

This was the first final to be played in front of a full attendance since the [[2019 UEFA Champions League final|2019 final]], as the previous two finals were affected by the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Europe|COVID-19 pandemic]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Bayern Munich Wins Champions League, a Victory for Tradition and Team |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/23/sports/soccer/psg-bayern-live-score.html |website=The New York Times |last=Smith |first=Rory |date=23 August 2020 |access-date=23 August 2020}}</ref> The final was originally scheduled to be played at the [[Allianz Arena]] in [[Munich]]. After the postponement and relocation of the [[2020 UEFA Champions League final|2020 final]], the final hosts were shifted back a year, so the 2022 final was given to the [[Krestovsky Stadium]] in [[Saint Petersburg]].<ref name="ExCo 17 June">{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/025e-0fb6101cfdbf-8b878372d79d-1000--uefa-competitions-to-resume-in-august/ |title=UEFA competitions to resume in August |work=UEFA.com |date=17 June 2020 |access-date=17 June 2020}}</ref> Following the [[2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine|Russian invasion of Ukraine]] on 24 February, however, UEFA called an extraordinary meeting of the executive committee a day later,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0272-14855c6e8982-98c47ce4b93b-1000/ |title=UEFA calls extraordinary meeting of the Executive Committee |work=UEFA.com |date=24 February 2022 |access-date=24 February 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/60504979 |title=Ukraine crisis: Uefa to move Champions League final after Russian invasion |work=BBC Sport |date=24 February 2022 |access-date=24 February 2022}}</ref> and the final was relocated to the Stade de France.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/02/25/sports/soccer/champions-league-final-paris-russia.html |title=Champions League final Will Be Played in Paris, Not Russia |access-date=25 February 2022 |website=The New York Times |date=25 February 2022 |last1=Panja |first1=Tariq}}</ref> Before the match, [[2022 UEFA Champions League final chaos|chaos descended following issues with crowd control]], which led to kickoff being delayed by 36 minutes.<ref name=":0" />

As winners, Real Madrid earned the right to play against the winners of the [[2021–22 UEFA Europa League]],  [[Eintracht Frankfurt]], in the [[2022 UEFA Super Cup]], and also qualified for the [[2022 FIFA Club World Cup]], in which they both won. While undetermined at the time of the final, its result also qualified the team for the [[2025 FIFA Club World Cup|first edition of the expanded FIFA Club World Cup in 2025]] using UEFA's champions pathway (The winners of the [[List of European Cup and UEFA Champions League finals|2021–2024 Champions Leagues]]), and increased [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]]'s chances of qualifying using the [[UEFA coefficient]] pathway (The top 8 clubs qualifying).<ref>{{Cite web |title=FIFA Council confirms key details for FIFA Club World Cup 2025 |url=https://www.fifa.com/about-fifa/organisation/fifa-council/media-releases/origin1904-p.cxm.fifa.com/fifa-council-confirms-key-details-for-fifa-club-world-cup-2025-tm |access-date=20 December 2023 |website=www.fifa.com |language=en}}</ref>

==Background==
[[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] played in their 10th European Cup/UEFA Champions League final. They had previously won six finals (in [[1977 European Cup final|1977]], [[1978 European Cup final|1978]], [[1981 European Cup final|1981]], [[1984 European Cup final|1984]], [[2005 UEFA Champions League final|2005]] and [[2019 UEFA Champions League final|2019]]) and lost three (in [[1985 European Cup final|1985]], [[2007 UEFA Champions League final|2007]] and [[2018 UEFA Champions League final|2018]]).<ref>{{cite web |first=Michael |last=Emons |title=Villarreal 2–3 Liverpool (2–5 on aggregate): Jurgen Klopp's side advance to Champions League final |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/61300681 |work=BBC Sport |date=3 May 2022 |access-date=26 May 2022 |archive-date=5 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220505164443/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/61300681 |url-status=live}}</ref> This was the fourth UEFA Champions League final for manager [[Jürgen Klopp]], after his loss with [[Borussia Dortmund]] in [[2013 UEFA Champions League final|2013]] and the Liverpool finals in 2018 and 2019 the latter of which he won.<ref>{{cite web |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Man City 2–0 Paris St-Germain (4–1 on aggregate): City into first Champions League final |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/56973031 |work=BBC Sport |date=4 May 2021 |access-date=7 May 2021 |archive-date=6 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210506200034/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/56973031 |url-status=live}}</ref> In addition to their six European Cup/UEFA Champions League titles, Liverpool also played in one [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|European Cup Winners' Cup]] final, losing in [[1966 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1966]] against Borussia Dortmund, and in four [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup/Europa League]] finals, winning in [[1973 UEFA Cup final|1973]], [[1976 UEFA Cup final|1976]] and [[2001 UEFA Cup final|2001]] and losing in [[2016 UEFA Europa League final|2016]]. Their manager [[Jürgen Klopp]] was seeking to become the fourth consecutive German to manage a Champions League-winning club.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/mediaservices/informationkits/competitions/uefachampionsleague/2022/match/2034586/ |title=UCL 2022 Liverpool – Real Madrid match facts |publisher=UEFA |access-date=30 May 2022}}</ref>

[[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] played in a record 17th European Cup/UEFA Champions League final, and the first since their win in [[2018 UEFA Champions League final|2018]] against Liverpool. They previously won 13 finals (in [[1956 European Cup final|1956]], [[1957 European Cup final|1957]], [[1958 European Cup final|1958]], [[1959 European Cup final|1959]], [[1960 European Cup final|1960]], [[1966 European Cup final|1966]], [[1998 UEFA Champions League final|1998]], [[2000 UEFA Champions League final|2000]], [[2002 UEFA Champions League final|2002]], [[2014 UEFA Champions League final|2014]], [[2016 UEFA Champions League final|2016]], [[2017 UEFA Champions League final|2017]] and 2018) and lost three ([[1962 European Cup final|1962]], [[1964 European Cup final|1964]] and 1981).<ref>{{cite web |first=Emlyn |last=Begley |title=Real Madrid 3–1 Manchester City (6–5 agg): Real come back to reach Champions League final |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/61312216 |work=BBC Sport |access-date=26 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220508145551/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/61312216 |archive-date=8 May 2022 |date=4 May 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref> Their manager [[Carlo Ancelotti]] reached a record fifth UEFA Champions League final as manager, winning in 2003 and 2007 and losing in 2005 while in charge of [[AC Milan|Milan]], and winning the 2014 final with Real Madrid, and was seeking to become the first manager in history to win four UEFA Champions League titles and eight European trophies.<ref>{{cite web |title=Real Madrid coach Carlo Ancelotti on the UEFA Champions League final against Liverpool and bid for more history – interview |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0275-153c1717ddd6-bee8be412fa9-1000--real-madrid-coach-carlo-ancelotti-on-the-uefa-champions-league-/ |publisher=UEFA |access-date=26 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526125047/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0275-153c1717ddd6-bee8be412fa9-1000--real-madrid-coach-carlo-ancelotti-on-the-uefa-champions-league-/ |archive-date=26 May 2022 |date=26 May 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |first=Alex |last=Bysouth |title=Carlo Ancelotti: What next for Real Madrid's record-breaking boss? |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/61310122 |work=BBC Sport |access-date=26 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220505203830/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/61310122 |archive-date=5 May 2022 |date=3 May 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref> Real Madrid also played in two [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|European Cup Winners' Cup]] finals (losing in [[1971 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1971]] and [[1983 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1983]]) and two [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]] finals (winning in [[1985 UEFA Cup final|1985]] and [[1986 UEFA Cup final|1986]]).<ref>{{cite web |title=Big Match Feature: Liverpool v Real Madrid |url=https://supersport.com/news/974649bd-a621-4c63-ab50-adfc1b41d110/big_match_feature_liverpool_v_real_madrid/ |website=Super Sport |access-date=29 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220529015558/https://supersport.com/news/974649bd-a621-4c63-ab50-adfc1b41d110/big_match_feature_liverpool_v_real_madrid/ |archive-date=29 May 2022 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

This was a record-third time that the two teams meet in the final, after the 1981 final, which was held in the [[Parc des Princes]] in Paris and in which Liverpool prevailed 1–0, and the 2018 final held at the [[Olimpiyskiy National Sports Complex]] in Kyiv, which was won 3–1 by Real Madrid. This was also the third final for Carlo Ancelotti as manager against Liverpool, after the 2005 and 2007 finals; he also lost the 1984 final as a player for [[AS Roma|Roma]]. This was the sixth time a Spanish side met an English team in the final of the competition, after the 1981, [[2006 UEFA Champions League final|2006]] (won by [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] against [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]]), the [[2009 UEFA Champions League final|2009]] and [[2011 UEFA Champions League final|2011]] finals (both won by Barcelona against [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]]), and the 2018 final.<ref name="RSSSF EC">{{Cite web |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |date=2 April 2020 |title=European Champions' Cup |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/ec1.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110208171501/http://rsssf.com/tablese/ec1.html |archive-date=8 February 2011 |access-date=30 May 2022 |website=[[RSSSF]]}}</ref>

Besides the 1981 and 2018 finals, the two clubs had met each other six times. Liverpool won both matches in the [[2008–09 UEFA Champions League knockout phase#Round of 16|2008–09 UEFA Champions League round of 16]] (1–0 away and 4–0 at home). The two clubs were drawn in the same group in the [[2014–15 UEFA Champions League group stage#Group B|2014–15 UEFA Champions League group stage]], with Real Madrid winning 3–0 away and 1–0 at home. In the [[2020–21 UEFA Champions League knockout phase#Quarter-finals|2020–21 quarter-finals]], the Spanish side won 3–1 at home and held a 0–0 draw at [[Anfield]] to ensure progression to the semi-finals.<ref>{{cite web |title=Liverpool vs Real Madrid Champions League final match facts: Previous meetings, pedigree, links and trivia, latest news |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0275-15281eca12ba-8c4b2a20b3c3-1000--liverpool-vs-real-madrid-champions-league-final-match-facts-pre/ |publisher=UEFA |access-date=26 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526130902/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0275-15281eca12ba-8c4b2a20b3c3-1000--liverpool-vs-real-madrid-champions-league-final-match-facts-pre/ |archive-date=26 May 2022 |date=25 May 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref>

