==Pre-match==
===Identity===
The original logo of the 2022 UEFA Champions League final at Saint Petersburg was unveiled at the group stage draw on 26 August 2021 in [[Istanbul]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/026d-134d790229e9-c21eaed49543-1000--2022-final-branding-unveiled/ |title=2022 UEFA Champions League final branding unveiled |work=UEFA.com |date=22 September 2021 |access-date=6 March 2022}}{{dead link|date=March 2025|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref> Originally, former [[Russia national football team|Russia]]n forward [[Andrey Arshavin]] was chosen to be the ambassador for the final. However, he was removed after the final's relocation to Saint-Denis.<ref>{{cite news |last=Thorogood |first=James |url=https://www.dw.com/en/champions-league-uefa-forced-into-round-of-16-redraw-bayern-draw-salzburg/a-60101754 |title=Champions League: UEFA forced into Round of 16 redraw, Bayern draw Salzburg |work=[[Deutsche Welle]] |date=13 December 2021 |access-date=15 March 2022}}</ref>

===Officials===
[[File:Clément Turpin 2016 (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright=.8|Frenchman [[Clément Turpin]] officiated the final.]]
On 11 May 2022, UEFA named Frenchman [[Clément Turpin]] as the [[Referee (association football)|referee]] for the final.<ref name="Referees">{{cite news |url=https://sports.yahoo.com/news/clement-turpin-appointed-champions-league-182556665.html |title=Clement Turpin appointed as Champions League final referee for Liverpool v Real Madrid clash |work=[[Yahoo! Sports]] |date=11 May 2022 |access-date=3 May 2022}}</ref> Turpin had been a [[FIFA International Referees List|FIFA referee]] since 2010, and was previously the fourth official in the [[2018 UEFA Champions League final]], also between Real Madrid and Liverpool. In the previous season he was the referee for the [[2021 UEFA Europa League Final]] between [[Villarreal CF|Villarreal]] and [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]]. He officiated eight prior matches in the 2021–22 Champions League season, with two matches in qualification, four in the group stage and two knockout fixtures. He served as a referee at the [[UEFA European Championship]] in [[UEFA Euro 2016|2016]] and [[UEFA Euro 2020|2020]], as well as at the [[2018 FIFA World Cup]] in Russia. Turpin also was a video assistant referee at the [[2017 FIFA Confederations Cup]] in Russia (including in the [[2017 FIFA Confederations Cup Final|final]]), the [[2017 FIFA Club World Cup]] in the United Arab Emirates and the [[2019 FIFA Women's World Cup]] in France

Turpin was joined by five of his fellow countrymen, including [[Assistant referee (association football)|assistant referees]] Nicolas Danos and Cyril Gringore.<ref name="Referees"/> [[Benoît Bastien]] served as the fourth official. [[Jérôme Brisard]] acted as the [[video assistant referee]] (VAR), with [[Willy Delajod]] appointed as one of the assistant VAR officials, along with Italian referees [[Massimiliano Irrati]] and Filippo Meli.<ref name="officials"/><ref name="Referees"/>

===Opening ceremony===
Cuban-born American singer [[Camila Cabello]] performed for the opening ceremony before the start of the match.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0275-151a623b63d1-34f72f8fe69f-1000/ |title=Camila Cabello to perform at the 2022 UEFA Champions League final Opening Ceremony, presented by Pepsi |work=UEFA.com |date=9 May 2022 |access-date=29 May 2022}}</ref> Cabello performed her songs "[[Señorita (Shawn Mendes and Camila Cabello song)|Señorita]]", "[[La Buena Vida (song)|La Buena Vida]]", "[[Havana (Camila Cabello song)|Havana]]", "[[Bam Bam (Camila Cabello song)|Bam Bam]]" and "[[Don't Go Yet]]".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sam |date=28 May 2022 |title=Watch: Camila Cabello Scores with UEFA Champions League Final Performance |url=https://thatgrapejuice.net/2022/05/watch-camila-cabello-scores-with-uefa-champions-league-final-performance/ |access-date=29 May 2022 |website=That Grape Juice}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Peters |first=Mitchell |date=28 May 2022 |title=Camila Cabello Pays Homage to Her Latin Heritage at Stunning UEFA Champions League Final Opening Ceremony |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/music-news/camila-cabello-uefa-champions-league-final-opening-ceremony-1235078758/ |access-date=29 May 2022 |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]}}</ref>

===Issues entering stadium and match delay===
{{Main|2022 UEFA Champions League final chaos}}
Crowd control descended into chaos at the entrances to the Stade de France prior to the beginning of the match.<ref>{{Cite news |date=29 May 2022 |title=Champions League final: UK calls for investigation into Paris stadium chaos |work=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-61626746}}</ref> By 21:00 local time, the originally scheduled kick-off time, thousands of seats remained empty in the Liverpool end. Ostensibly for security reasons, the kick-off was initially delayed by 15 minutes to 21:15. This was further delayed by 15 minutes to 21:30, before being pushed back another six minutes to 21:36. The match eventually started at 21:37.<ref name=":0">{{cite web |title=Liverpool 'hugely disappointed' by treatment of fans outside Paris final |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2022/may/28/champions-league-final-kick-off-delay-liverpool-real-madrid-paris |access-date=29 May 2022 |website=The Guardian |date=28 May 2022}}</ref>  

France's Interior Minister at the time, [[Gérald Darmanin]], later admitted that security arrangements for the final were wrong, and that his first public remarks – blaming Liverpool fans – were a mistake. "It was a failure because I had not foreseen. That was a mistake on my part. I was led astray by my preconceptions," he said, admitting that "The scapegoat was easy to find, and I apologise now to Liverpool supporters. They were quite right to be hurt. It was a mistake and a failure."<ref>{{Cite web | url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ckg2vyxzg3qo#:~:text=French%20minister%20apologises%20to%20Liverpool%20fans%20over%20Champions%20League%20chaos,-Reuters&text=France's%20former%20interior%20minister%20has,wrongly%20blamed%20for%20a%20riot. | title=French minister apologises to Liverpool fans over Champions League chaos | website=www.bbc.co.uk}}</ref>

==Match==
===Summary===
[[File:Thibaut Courtois at the 2018 World Cup (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright|[[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] goalkeeper [[Thibaut Courtois]] was named [[man of the match]] after making a total of nine saves in the match.]]

====First half====
The match kicked off at the delayed time of 21:36 in front of a crowd of 75,000. In the 16th minute, [[Trent Alexander-Arnold]] played the ball across the Real Madrid penalty area which ended up being hit towards goal by [[Mohamed Salah]] before being saved by [[Thibaut Courtois]] for the first effort of the match.<ref name="SportingNews">{{cite web |last=Farrell |first=Dom |url=https://www.sportingnews.com/us/soccer/news/liverpool-real-madrid-score-highlights-champions-league/kgexl1rv7m8yx3sm4duprcca |title=Liverpool vs. Real Madrid result, highlights and analysis as Vinicius and Courtois inspire Los Blancos to 14th European title |work=Sporting News |date=28 May 2022 |access-date=29 May 2022}}</ref> Five minutes later, [[Thiago Alcântara]] played a ball through to [[Sadio Mané]] who managed to make space for himself and take a shot which was saved on to the left post by Courtois. In the 34th minute, Alexander-Arnold played another cross into the Madrid penalty area towards Salah, who managed to direct a header towards goal but straight at Courtois.<ref name="SportingNews"/> 

Two minutes before half-time, [[Karim Benzema]] made a clear run towards the Liverpool penalty area on the right and managed to take the ball under control before attempting to pass the ball across before being cut out by [[Ibrahima Konaté]]. However, a mix up between the Liverpool defender as well as goalkeeper [[Alisson]] allowed [[Federico Valverde]] to try to step in to intercept, with Liverpool midfielder [[Fabinho (footballer, born 1993)|Fabinho]] also forced to intervene.<ref name="guardian1"/><ref name="nyt"/> Among the attempts to win the ball, it ended up falling to Benzema again who then passed the ball into the net. However, the goal was disallowed initially by the assistant referee and then also by the VAR after a check of three minutes and 20 seconds.<ref>{{cite web |last=Johnson |first=Dale |url=https://www.espn.co.uk/football/uefa-champions-league/story/4677421/why-karim-benzemas-goal-was-offside-for-real-madrid-in-the-champions-league-final |title=Why Karim Benzema's goal was offside for Real Madrid in the Champions League final |work=ESPN |date=30 May 2022 |access-date=3 June 2022}}</ref> Benzema was shown to be in an offside position when the ball came to him, however there was controversy as to whether the last touch off of Fabinho was intentional or not.<ref>{{cite web |last=Winehouse |first=Amitai |title=Why was Karim Benzema's goal ruled out during Liverpool vs Real Madrid? |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/4176613/2022/05/28/why-was-karim-benzemas-goal-ruled-out-during-liverpool-vs-real-madrid/ |website=The Athletic |date=29 May 2022 |access-date=29 May 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title='I really don't like that' – talkSPORT hosts disagree over Karim Benzema's controversial disallowed goal in Liverpool's Champions League clash with Real Madrid |url=https://talksport.com/football/1119875/karim-benzema-liverpool-real-madrid/ |website=talkSPORT |date=28 May 2022 |access-date=29 May 2022}}</ref> There were three minutes of stoppage time.<ref name="guardian1">{{cite web |last=Smyth |first=Rob |title=Real Madrid beat Liverpool 1–0 in Champions League final |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2022/may/28/champions-league-final-liverpool-real-madrid-live-updates |website=The Guardian |date=28 May 2022 |access-date=29 May 2022}}</ref>

====Second half====
[[File:Vinicius Jr 2021.jpg|thumb|upright|[[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]]'s [[Vinícius Júnior]] scored the lone goal of the match in the 59th minute.]]
Neither side made any line-up changes during the interval.<ref name="espn">{{cite web |title=Real Madrid beat Liverpool, win 2021–22 Champions League: How the final played out |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/uefa-champions-league/story/4674348/live-champions-league-final-liverpoolreal-madrid-clash-in-paris |website=ESPN |date=28 May 2022 |access-date=29 May 2022}}</ref> Liverpool continued where they left off, as nine minutes into the second half Alexander-Arnold played in another cross towards the Real Madrid penalty area which was not far from the head of Alcântara and ended up being punched away by Courtois.<ref name="SportingNews"/> In the 59th minute, Madrid took the lead against the run of play; Valverde made a run with the ball on the right hand side, before eventually playing a low cross towards [[Vinícius Júnior]] in the penalty area who managed to tuck the ball into the net to make it 1–0, having been left unmarked and Alisson left stranded on the other side of the goal.<ref name="guardian1" /><ref name="nyt" />

Fabinho received the first and only booking of the night, after being shown a yellow card in the 62nd minute by referee [[Clément Turpin]] for a challenge on Valverde.<ref name="guardian1" /> With 24 minutes remaining, Liverpool pressed for an equaliser. Salah came forward with the ball and tried a shot from just outside the penalty area which ended up being saved to the right side of his goal by Courtois.<ref name="SportingNews"/> Liverpool then made their first substitution of the final in the 65th minute, as [[Luis Díaz (footballer, born 1997)|Luis Díaz]] came off for [[Diogo Jota]].<ref name="nyt" /><ref name="guardian1" /> Liverpool pushed again for a goal as a header across the face of goal by Jota almost found its way towards Salah, but was blocked off by Courtois.<ref name="SportingNews"/> Madrid then had a chance to score another in the 75th minute, as a free kick into the Liverpool penalty area by [[Toni Kroos]] found its way to [[Casemiro]] who then tried to play the ball across towards Benzema and Vinícius, but was collected by [[Andrew Robertson]].<ref name="guardian2">{{cite web |title=Real Madrid beat Liverpool 1–0 in Champions League Final |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2022/may/28/champions-league-final-liverpool-real-madrid-live-updates?filterKeyEvents=false |website=The Guardian |date=28 May 2022 |access-date=29 May 2022}}</ref><ref name="nyt">{{cite news |title=Champions League Final: Real Madrid Beats Liverpool for 14th Title |url=https://www.nytimes.com/live/2022/05/28/sports/champions-league-final |website=The New York Times |date=28 May 2022 |access-date=29 May 2022 |last1=Smith |first1=Rory}}</ref>

Liverpool then made their two more changes, as Alcântara and captain [[Jordan Henderson]] came off for [[Naby Keïta]] and [[Roberto Firmino]] in the 77th minute.<ref name="guardian2" /><ref name="nyt" /> Liverpool continued to push for the equaliser, as passing took place between Salah and Firmino before Salah took another shot from just outside the penalty area which ended up being deflected off of Jota and carefully beaten away by Courtois down his bottom left.<ref name="SportingNews"/> Salah came forward again with eight minutes left, as a ball over the top from Fabinho found its way to the Egyptian who tried to battle off [[Ferland Mendy]] before coming across and having his resulting effort be saved by Courtois.<ref name="SportingNews"/> Madrid made their first change of the match in the 86th minute, as Valverde came off for [[Eduardo Camavinga]].<ref name="guardian2" /> At the end of regular time, the fourth official displayed that a minimum of five minutes of stoppage time would be played.<ref name="guardian2" /><ref name="nyt" /> Madrid made their two other substitutions in stoppage time, as [[Dani Ceballos]] came on for [[Luka Modrić]] in the 90th minute and goalscorer Vinícius was brought off in the 93rd minute for [[Rodrygo]].<ref name="guardian2" /> Despite Liverpool's continued pushes for a goal in the final moments, the match was brought to an end with Madrid winning 1–0.<ref name="guardian2" />

