==Post-match==
With their win, Real Madrid secured a record-extending 14th title in the European Cup/Champions League. The club has won twice as many titles as the next-best team, [[AC Milan|Milan]] (7 titles).<ref>{{Cite web |last=Marcotti |first=Gabrielle |date=28 May 2022 |title=Real Madrid embellish Champions League legacy in beating Liverpool for 14th European title |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/uefa-champions-league/story/4676133/real-madrid-embellish-champions-league-legacy-in-beating-liverpool-for-14th-european-title |access-date=29 May 2022 |website=ESPN}}</ref> This was Madrid's fifth title in nine seasons (since [[2013–14 UEFA Champions League|2013–14]]), their eighth consecutive win in a Champions League final, and their tenth successive victory in a major European final (including the [[1985 UEFA Cup Final|1985]] and [[1986 UEFA Cup Final]]s). Real Madrid has not lost a final in the Champions League era, with their last defeat occurring in 1981.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Champions League final by the numbers: The best stats and facts as Real Madrid claim their 14th title at Liverpool's expense |url=https://www.sportingnews.com/us/soccer/news/champions-league-final-stats-real-madrid-liverpool/kdbbzghcdfaknucb94vmatjq |access-date=29 May 2022 |website=www.sportingnews.com |date=28 May 2022}}</ref> Real Madrid also claimed their fourth [[Double (association football)#Continental double|European double]] (after [[1956–57 Real Madrid CF season|1956–57]], [[1957–58 Real Madrid CF season|1957–58]] and [[2016–17 Real Madrid CF season|2016–17]]).<ref>{{Cite web |date=28 May 2022 |title=Real Madrid beats Liverpool 1–0 for 14th European Cup title |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2022/05/28/real-madrid-beats-liverpool-1-0-for-14th-european-cup-title.html |access-date=29 May 2022 |website=CNBC}}</ref> With the win, Spanish teams extended their streak of 17 straight major European finals won against non-Spanish opposition.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://en.as.com/soccer/champions-league-final-2022-liverpool-vs-real-madrid-live-online-goals-plays-updates-n/ |title=Real Madrid wins the Champions League final against Liverpool: summary, goals, plays... |work=[[Diario AS]] |date=28 May 2022 |access-date=29 May 2022}}</ref> Real Madrid goalkeeper [[Thibaut Courtois]] was selected as the man of the match,<ref name="MOTM"/> having made nine saves in the game, a record in the final since [[Opta Sports|Opta]] began keeping records in [[2003–04 UEFA Champions League|2003–04]]. His 59 saves during the campaign also set a new Champions League single-season record since Opta began keeping records.<ref>{{cite web |last=Booth |first=Chuck |url=https://www.cbssports.com/soccer/news/champions-league-real-madrid-leaned-on-thibaut-courtois-all-tournament-long-he-saved-the-best-for-last/ |title=Champions League: Real Madrid leaned on Thibaut Courtois all tournament long, he saved the best for last |work=[[CBS Sports]] |date=28 May 2022 |access-date=29 May 2022}}</ref>

[[Carlo Ancelotti]] became the first manager to win four Champions League titles, having previously won the [[2003 UEFA Champions League final|2003]] and [[2007 UEFA Champions League final|2007]] tournaments with Milan, and the [[2014 UEFA Champions League final|2014]] title with Madrid.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0275-15407684c988-5b733e851bf5-1000/ |title=Champions League-winning coaches: Carlo Ancelotti becomes first four-time winner |work=UEFA.com |date=28 May 2022 |access-date=29 May 2022}}</ref> Conversely, Jürgen Klopp tied [[Marcello Lippi]] for most losses in Champions League finals. Klopp had previously lost with [[Borussia Dortmund]] in [[2013 UEFA Champions League final|2013]] and Liverpool in [[2018 UEFA Champions League final|2018]].<ref name="UCL Final Stats">{{cite web |date=29 May 2022 |title=UCL Final Stats: Benzema, Modric join Ronaldo; Real Madrid's uneclipsed 14 |url=https://www.espn.in/football/blog-espn-fc-united/story/4676828/ucl-final-stats-benzema-modric-join-ronaldo-real-madrid-uneclipsed-14 |access-date=29 May 2022 |website=ESPN}}</ref> Nine players secured their fifth Champions League title with the win, with eight ([[Gareth Bale]], [[Karim Benzema]], [[Dani Carvajal]], [[Casemiro]], [[Isco]], [[Marcelo (footballer, born 1988)|Marcelo]], [[Luka Modrić]] and [[Nacho (footballer, born 1990)|Nacho]]) doing so with Real Madrid's wins in 2014 and from 2016 to 2018. [[Toni Kroos]] won his first title with [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] in [[2013 UEFA Champions League final|2013]], before winning with Real Madrid from 2016 to 2018. They joined former Real Madrid player [[Cristiano Ronaldo]] as the only players to have won five titles in the Champions League era.<ref name="UCL Final Stats"/>

===Subsequent meetings===
The two teams would meet again in the [[2022–23 UEFA Champions League|next edition]] of the Champions League, in the round of 16. In the first leg at [[Anfield]], Liverpool took a 2–0 lead within 14 minutes through a backheel goal from summer signing [[Darwin Núñez]], before [[Mohamed Salah]] capitalised on a slip from Real Madrid goalkeeper Thibaut Courtois. However, they could not hold on to it and lost 5–2 thanks to two goals from the previous final's match-winner [[Vinícius Júnior]] and [[Karim Benzema]], and one from [[Éder Militão]].<ref>{{cite news |last=McNulty |first=Phil |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/64708314 |title=Liverpool 2-5 Real Madrid: Hosts thrashed by holders in Champions League last 16 |work=BBC Sport |date=15 March 2023 |access-date=17 February 2025}}</ref> In the second leg at the [[Santiago Bernabéu Stadium]], Benzema scored the only goal of the match to give Real Madrid a 1–0 win, 6–2 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite news |last=Begley |first=Emlyn |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/64959699 |title=Real Madrid 1-0 Liverpool (6-2 agg): Reds dumped out of Champions League |work=BBC Sport |date=15 March 2023 |access-date=17 February 2025}}</ref>

The two would then meet at Anfield in the [[2024–25 UEFA Champions League]] [[2024–25 UEFA Champions League league phase|league phase]]. This time, Liverpool were under the management of [[Arne Slot]], following Jürgen Klopp's decision to leave the club at the end of the [[2023–24 Liverpool F.C. season|2023–24]] season.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.liverpoolfc.com/news/jurgen-klopp-announces-decision-step-down-liverpool-manager-end-season |title=Jürgen Klopp announces decision to step down as Liverpool manager at end of season |work=Liverpool F.C. |date=26 January 2024 |access-date=17 February 2025}}</ref> Meanwhile, Real Madrid were the holders of the competition, having won a record-extending 15th European Cup/Champions League, in a similar case to their meeting in 2023. Liverpool won the match 2–0, through goals from [[Alexis Mac Allister]] and [[Cody Gakpo]]. Both sides saw missed penalties; Real Madrid's [[Kylian Mbappé]] saw his penalty saved by [[Caoimhin Kelleher]], before Liverpool's Mohamed Salah sent his effort just wide.

The teams met once again in the [[2025–26 UEFA Champions League]] league phase at Anfield where Liverpool managed a 1-0 win thanks to a headed goal from [[Alexis Mac Allister]] from a [[Dominik Szoboszlai]] free kick.

